        "Charles L. Kane",
        "Eugene J. Mele",
        "Laurens W. Molenkamp"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Time-reversal-invariant topological insulators and the quantum spin Hall effect revealed a new organizing principle for electronic states and stimulated a broad, durable field of materials research.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele supplied the central theoretical ideas; Molenkamp's experiments established the quantum spin Hall effect in a material system.",
        "maturity": "The prediction and experimental demonstrations have been extensively followed by confirmations and many subsequent topological materials discoveries.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The first experimental system had important limitations, and credit for the wider topological-insulator field extends beyond these foundational contributions."
        ]
      }
    },
